{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064284 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064129 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "4ebd0289-b278-5352-b7bd-10ae218c43fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-735" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y = \\tan{\\left(\\sin(x)\\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\tan u, \\qquad u = \\sin x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\sec^2 u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048465}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484798 :question-generator/id #uuid "33d29e3d-bb12-5c9e-a20a-5ea0e9d6624e" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186064284.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8138f99-7ec9-5256-bd35-e33c7e848dfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x\\cot^{2} (\\cos x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062468} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe44e20-3664-5950-8af4-4d1829f581e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x\\csc^{2} (\\cos x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062469} {:answer/id #uuid "ecca65a3-9b8e-59f8-b57a-ab7326ffb1c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\csc x\\sin^{2} (\\cos x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062470} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9ff75e-9b68-51c0-ab7d-23b5bb2fa18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x\\cos^{2} (\\csc x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062471} {:answer/id #uuid "679060c0-1bb6-505c-82ad-9ea21f50f18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\csc x\\cos^{2} (\\sin x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062472}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5fb1db77-5782-5f86-9fa6-af75143f4888" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062467}] :question/id #uuid "2988d73e-07d4-547b-8f00-c916e255eb6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-31356" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y = \\cot (\\cos x)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b27ffe2-7676-50cf-8f85-63acc6816398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327316} {:answer/id #uuid "446323eb-ee99-55ba-b53a-c33b7f83bceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327531} {:answer/id #uuid "6e31cdb3-4a26-586a-a124-b350d2c5b39e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327889} {:answer/id #uuid "a5306166-8267-56b7-84f3-d958abd6020b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328359} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3ebbc8-ea7e-59c1-b950-2b9dc34de1a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "446323eb-ee99-55ba-b53a-c33b7f83bceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327531} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5003d8b-4b77-55dc-a36e-b7375c9d128f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55ba3ddf-3b81-51ce-a46b-d6facbbbea67" :question/math-academy-id "q-48643" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9728f53e-cf89-532c-9077-996c48020b2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-48644" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y =\\cos \\left(\\sin x \\right)$ at the point $\\left(0,1 \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 0$ is given by $f'(0).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\nf = \\cos{u}, \\qquad u = \\sin x.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = -\\sin{u}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =\\cos x.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n&=-\\sin u \\cdot (\\cos x)\\\\[5pt]\n&=-\\cos x \\sin (\\sin x).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we find\n\\[\n\t\\eqalign{\n\tf'(0)&=-\\cos (0) \\sin (\\sin (0))\\\\[3pt]\n\t&=-1 \\cdot 0 \\\\[3pt]\n        &=0.\n}\n\\]\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $(0,1) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n\\begin{align}\ny-y_1 &= m(x-x_1)\\\\[3pt]\ny-1 &= (0)\\cdot\\left(x-0\\right)\\\\[3pt]\ny&=1.\n\\end{align}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3114b1b-5508-592e-9c4c-63d9fe2d48cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-72163" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=\\operatorname{sin}(\\operatorname{sec}x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}u,\\,u=\\operatorname{sec}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}u\\cdot (\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\operatorname{sec}x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6630c4de-45f8-525a-9088-43f567b2047c" :question/math-academy-id "q-72170" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y =\\csc ( \\cos x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\csc{u}, \\qquad u = \\cos{x}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = -\\csc{u}\\cot u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\sin{x}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=-\\csc u \\cot u \\cdot  (-\\sin x)\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\sin x\\csc \\left(\\cos x\\right) \\cot \\left(\\cos x\\right).\n\\end{align*}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc286831-208d-5612-9363-4e8460161c19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc286831-208d-5612-9363-4e8460161c19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313569} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f72dc37-b228-560d-aba6-e98b5e17af4d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314057} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "231f91ac-3de6-59a3-862f-b8057c01af56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "231f91ac-3de6-59a3-862f-b8057c01af56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312235} :answer-field/id #uuid "a272334b-048a-545c-b7bd-74f9c4889490" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76019817-2e4b-589f-ae43-b8959040e2aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-341223" :question/problem "Given that $y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)),$ we have\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u),\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317264 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b3638de-2232-5b1b-8cad-82466c730a8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}(\\operatorname{tan}(x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b3638de-2232-5b1b-8cad-82466c730a8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}(\\operatorname{tan}(x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315180} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8797a85-728c-5c21-bc99-32e06307e7d7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6aceca48-ebb2-5f8b-b98b-8c687aa13f66" :question/math-academy-id "q-342436" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)),$ we have\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u),\\,u=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={sec}^{2}⁡(u),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={sec}^{2}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(x) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b31fbe-14ab-5c3d-8b9a-02859f8e2640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23b31fbe-14ab-5c3d-8b9a-02859f8e2640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315317} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a4e064c-845a-59e5-8333-2b01b7ac8e6d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316902} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "937de51f-ef0a-5577-95ef-6a8fef931e80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "937de51f-ef0a-5577-95ef-6a8fef931e80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315940} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad5dcd71-ae03-574c-b8dd-2c78211d0666" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316953} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a7553de-ae66-58dc-a54c-8fcb1941ce52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a7553de-ae66-58dc-a54c-8fcb1941ce52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315454} :answer-field/id #uuid "f850e3ea-810b-5928-bf54-2533be4452a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50d04d8e-3b54-51a1-977a-8035c4a5ed2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-342443" :question/problem "Given that $y=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)),$ we have\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sec}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)\\operatorname{tan}⁡($\n{{field-3}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u),\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd80f4fc-d66d-5918-905b-16bb0d321fbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd80f4fc-d66d-5918-905b-16bb0d321fbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316535} :answer-field/id #uuid "28742ecc-1707-5ebf-af66-6910320113a0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316640} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb68a3a2-59aa-56c5-bc1b-c4aa4acceb78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb68a3a2-59aa-56c5-bc1b-c4aa4acceb78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316268} :answer-field/id #uuid "95746905-c009-54be-9389-495c9c01a41a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36a01249-dd25-5201-9ce2-0ee325c40559" :question/math-academy-id "q-342445" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)),$ we have\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x))\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u),\\,u=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x))\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ad365d4-359a-52c4-805a-c1687b51bf98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336430} {:answer/id #uuid "646e64f6-f63d-5208-9dad-bae8b2423826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336932} {:answer/id #uuid "785f85d3-99ed-5869-bb7a-14c4604ec1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{csc}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337115} {:answer/id #uuid "7d2e4cd6-fb63-51c7-b9f4-288b7402ef9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{csc}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337160} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ac1cf5-ef67-52e6-b531-518c01f08c90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "646e64f6-f63d-5208-9dad-bae8b2423826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336932} :answer-field/id #uuid "18999e45-9b70-57cd-8509-bc4dc9842011" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "363aed62-f3dd-56da-b30d-2474bf68073a" :question/math-academy-id "q-72161" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-{csc}^{2}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35d0b5a0-960c-537b-b36b-f81b09719172" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336532} {:answer/id #uuid "8659cf38-7f28-521b-9eb6-a1f4bb5d55a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337248} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2b4496-4d43-50c5-b8f4-50cbe3748941" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337845} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a21dd0-44ef-54d6-bc1c-1c98a20cbcd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338037} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b439b8-4fb4-5bd2-b82d-c5283f54f75b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7b439b8-4fb4-5bd2-b82d-c5283f54f75b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338204} :answer-field/id #uuid "76164faf-c99c-514d-8f55-17d4f55b16c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bdadc1fd-0dca-59ce-bd49-88a477cdb0a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-72173" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡u,\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd535f0f-13a3-59e8-aa98-e4bebe36408f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd535f0f-13a3-59e8-aa98-e4bebe36408f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484791} :answer-field/id #uuid "939e86f3-e069-59f1-87f9-c70b1bca8329" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "30a0323d-00da-5727-8bed-8f4570018874" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\cos{\\left(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{4} \\right)}$ at the point $\\left(0,- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)$.\n\nWrite the right-hand side of the equation in terms of $x$.\n\n$y=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{4})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $f'(0)=-\\sin(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4})=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a1c429d-91c5-5bb7-af02-633485681a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a1c429d-91c5-5bb7-af02-633485681a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484788} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf12cc56-7e9d-5f32-968f-6c8272d67936" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "53218be1-6a3c-5560-9338-ef81bd9ab181" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\cos{\\left(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}$ at the point $\\left(0,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)$.\n\nWrite the right-hand side of the equation in terms of $x$.\n\n$y=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{6}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{6})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $f'(0)=-\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{6})=- \\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bac1832-cbc0-5239-b224-d88d18d436fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bac1832-cbc0-5239-b224-d88d18d436fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484789} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c500a86-e1db-5337-a98a-9655641e4016" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9c2a70b8-20d0-5b7c-a81b-d0ca3277e712" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\cos{\\left(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right)}$ at the point $\\left(0,\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$.\n\nWrite the right-hand side of the equation in terms of $x$.\n\n$y=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{3}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{3})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $f'(0)=-\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{3})=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}.$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76829e80-fc0b-5c20-83fa-559e054cce93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76829e80-fc0b-5c20-83fa-559e054cce93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484790} :answer-field/id #uuid "f03b9128-e960-5087-b4b2-f78af1a3ef3c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cfc21866-9683-5244-ad3b-eca7c1a8e833" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\cos{\\left(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}$ at the point $\\left(0,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)$.\n\nWrite the right-hand side of the equation in terms of $x$.\n\n$y=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{4}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{4})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $f'(0)=-\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{4})=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484803 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cffb8108-0a8b-529c-831d-d01364b7996c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cffb8108-0a8b-529c-831d-d01364b7996c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484792} :answer-field/id #uuid "8aec45a1-2e42-5a0f-985b-263a94020dc4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f5d87654-628a-541b-9ab2-cf93106a65ac" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\cos{\\left(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{2 \\pi}{3} \\right)}$ at the point $\\left(0,- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)$.\n\nWrite the right-hand side of the equation in terms of $x$.\n\n$y=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{2 \\pi}{3})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $f'(0)=-\\sin(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3})=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}.$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}
